    "turn_text": "over. But he could win. He could win. He could win in part because they're trying to disenfranchise people, particularly people of color, and they are setting it up such that if he loses, they can overturn the election. And this is how democracies come to an end. It's not always through violent means. If there is another attack on the Capitol and there may very well be, that attack will fail, just like the last one. But where they may succeed is in these efforts to replace the Raffensburgers of the world and and have partisans there. You know, he was trying to get, you know, local elections officials of Michigan, state legislators in Pennsylvania, and state-wide officers in Georgia to essentially lie about the election. He may have people in those positions who are willing to do it next time. And that's how the democracies, you know, come to an end through the use of quasi-legal, quasi-democratic means to attack democracy itself. But before people get too gloomy about the prospects, I want to also say, and I chose the title of Midnight in Washington because well Midnight is the darkest hour of every day, it also is hopeful because what follows has the prospect of light. I fully believe that we're going to get through this, because I believe that there are millions and millions more Americans who love and cherish our democracy than those that are willing to tear it down. And I try to profile in the book all the heroes, not just the villains of this period, but the heroes who defied the president, who risked their jobs in some cases, risked their lives. People like Alex Ender-Vinman and people like",
